<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Viet Minh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Viet Minh was a Vietnamese independence movement founded in 1941 under the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a>. It fought against Japanese occupation and then against the restoration of French colonial rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> note its decisive victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> in 1954, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vo-nguyen-giap">General Vo Nguyen Giap</a>, which ended French rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viet-minh.jpg" alt="Viet Minh" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File History below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Vietnam_(1945%E2%80%931955).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Viet Minh, officially the League for Independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, was founded on 19 May 1941 at Pác Bó in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-bang">Cao Bằng</a> province by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a> set it up as a united front to achieve independence, the first step in a communist revolutionary project, and presented it as a broad coalition. Võ Nguyên Giáp commanded its military side, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-duan">Lê Duẩn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pham-van-dong">Phạm Văn Đồng</a> were among the other founders. Non-communists also joined, including the Democratic Party and the Socialist Party, as did most Buddhists. Many Catholics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caodaism">Cao Đài</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoa-hao">Hòa Hảo</a> followers first supported it but later split.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Viet Minh fought the Japanese occupiers with support from the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and later the American Office of Strategic Services. At the end of 1944 it claimed 500,000 members. After the collapse of Japanese authority and the abdication of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> proclaimed the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> on 2 September 1945. French and British forces attacked in the south from 23 September, and talks with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> broke down in December 1946, leading to nearly ten years of war.</p><p>The French pushed the Viet Minh out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, but their encirclement of the Việt Bắc base in late 1947 failed to defeat it. In 1950 the newly communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> provided bases and heavy weapons, and the Viet Minh took control of many rural areas. On 7 March 1951 it merged with the National United League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> to form the Vietnamese United Front, which people still called the Viet Minh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After its victory at Điện Biên Phủ, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> entered negotiations, and the Geneva Conference divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> at the 17th Parallel, with the Viet Minh army withdrawing to the North. Civil administration of North Vietnam passed to it on 11 October 1954, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> became prime minister. The promised unification elections of 1956 never took place, and Viet Minh cadres left in the South later took up an insurgency.</p><p>The United Front became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnamese-fatherland-front">Vietnam Fatherland Front</a> on 10 September 1955, today regarded as the modern incarnation of the Viet Minh. Its Vietnam Liberation Army, founded in 1944, is today the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-army-of-vietnam">People's Army of Vietnam</a>. Cambodian and Laotian movements, among them the Khmer Việt Minh and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathet-lao">Pathet Lao</a>, received training and support from it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Viet Minh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Carried out from 1953 to 1956, it affected more than 4 million rural people, and rent reduction nearly 8 million; most land was given out without payment, with priority for <b>Viet Minh</b> fighters and their families.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The catastrophic defeat that broke the second empire came at Dien Bien Phu in May 1954, where General Vo Nguyen Giap's <b>Viet Minh</b> besieged a French garrison and forced its surrender after fifty-six days.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the First Indochina War from 1946, a French Union force of up to several hundred thousand men, including Vietnamese, Laotian and Cambodian troops, fought the <b>Viet Minh</b>, losing the frontier posts in 1950 and suffering final defeat at Dien Bien Phu in May 1954.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Cambodia achieved independence under Norodom Sihanouk on 9 November 1953, though the communist United Issarak Front and the <b>Viet Minh</b> remained active; according to the historian David P. Chandler, leftist Issarak groups aided by the Viet Minh controlled as much as half the country on the eve of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970">Kingdom of Cambodia (1953-1970) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Japan’s surrender the <b>Viet Minh</b> seized power in the August Revolution, and on 30 August 1945 Bao Dai abdicated at Hue, handing the imperial seal and sword to the new government and ending the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 17 August 1945 the song was sung for the first time in public, at a rally of civil servants in Hanoi under the flag of the <b>Viet Minh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tien-quan-ca">Tiến quân ca</a></li></ul></section>
